#---
# purpose: "a module body cannot invoke a macro the same module defines"
# expect: diagnostic
# diagnostic.code: macro/own-module-item-call
#---
#
# # module_local_item_macro
#
# `Provider` defines `make_answer/0` and then invokes it in its own body. The
# module is still being defined at that point, so its own macros do not exist
# to call yet, and the call is refused where the item macro is classified.
#
# Elixir draws the same line. On this program it reports `undefined function
# make_answer/0 (there is no such import)` and refuses to compile the module,
# because a module's macros become callable only once the module is compiled.
# A function body of that same module may call them — by then it is.
#
# The way to write this is `provider_macros_item_macro`: put the macro in
# another module and import it.
#
defmodule Provider do
  def helper(x), do: x + 1

  defmacro make_answer() do
    body = quote do: Provider.helper(41)
    {:def, %{}, [{:answer, %{}, []}, [{:do, body}]]}
  end

  make_answer()
end

def main() do
  assert(Provider.answer() == 42, "unreachable: the module body is refused")
end
